          "stable_key": "core.big_o_notation",
          "title": "Big-O Notation",
          "est_effort_min": 60,
          "priority": 5,
          "depends_on": [],
          "diagnostic_probe": "What does it mean for an algorithm to run in O(n log n) time?",
          "diagnostic_answer": "It means the algorithm's runtime grows proportionally to n times the logarithm of n as the input size n increases — for example, a well-implemented comparison-based sort like mergesort. It's faster than O(n^2) but slower than O(n) for large inputs."
        },
        {
          "stable_key": "core.arrays_and_strings",
          "title": "Arrays & Strings",
          "est_effort_min": 75,
          "priority": 5,
          "depends_on": ["core.big_o_notation"]
        },
        {
          "stable_key": "core.hashmaps",
          "title": "Hash Maps",
          "est_effort_min": 75,
          "priority": 5,
          "depends_on": ["core.arrays_and_strings"],
          "diagnostic_probe": "Why is a hash map lookup typically O(1) on average?",
          "diagnostic_answer": "A good hash function distributes keys roughly evenly across buckets, so each bucket holds a small, roughly constant number of items — looking one up means computing its hash and checking that one bucket, independent of how many total items are stored."
        },
        {
